A||||11||ferg||Need help with rear struts||||1||
Z||000000||ferg||07-31-2001||08:35 PM||||I recently replaced my rear struts w/ KYB's. After taking the car to the alignment shop, the guy there says he can't align the right rear. He said the camber (sidewall of tire in/out relative to the ground)  was out 2 degrees, and they can't move the strut any more.<P>Is it possible that when replacing the strut- I managed to put the strut in wrong? I think the top only goes in one way, is this correct?<P>What other causes would cause the car to be out that much? <P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||07-31-2001||09:08 PM||||I'm guessing you have an '88 Fiero.  On some '88s the hole in the spindle and/or the strut needs to be elongated to allow for additional adjustment.<P>Remove the strut and grind a little material off of the strut and the spindle and realign.<P>Good luck.   ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ferg||07-31-2001||09:34 PM||||Actually have an 86 GT. I didn't talk to the guy, my dad did. The guy said they couldn't get the strut to move. I'm going to guess he was referring to moving the strut inwards- towards the engine and they ran out of adjustment. The tech said he needed to move the top of the tire inwards and the bottom of the tire outwards. I really don't know how I could've put the strut in wrong. Like I said in my first post- I thought they went in only one way. <P>Besides that- I put the left side in- and that side was fine. I'm lost as to why they can't align it. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Vader||07-31-2001||09:36 PM||||I have seen at least two Fieros that have had significant corrosion of the upper space frame stiffeners, and allowed the spring towers to move under loads and stresses.  Check the void above the inner fender liner and see if the stamped steel reinforcement rail is rusted out.  They can be replaced with heavier metal, but it's a real chore.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||07-31-2001||09:39 PM||||Did they charge you for the ALIGNMENT? if so take it to another shop and see what they say<BR>Just a thought???<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||3
Z||000005||mrfiero||07-31-2001||09:43 PM||||I have only heard of this problem with '88 Fieros and KYB's (which is why KYB doesn't list a strut for '88 Fieros).<P>Different year, same problem.  You probably still need to grind off some material from either the hole in the strut of the hole in the spindle (or both) to get enough movement to get the proper alignmemt.<P>There is no way to install the strut wrong, so the problem lies with the strut.  I guarantee that you can remove the right strut and install it on the left side and it will fit just fine and the left strut will have the same issues when installed on the right.<P>I can't help you with the rust thing, like Vader said.    ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ferg||07-31-2001||09:53 PM||||they charged me $39 for the partial alignment.<P>Going to take it to another shop to see if they can get it in specs.<P>I didn't think I put it in wrong- just had to be reassured.<P>I might have the shop grind some the metal off the strut tower, if that'll fix the problem. <P>I don't think rust is a problem- I haven't looked, but the rest of the underside is really clean- and w/ only 50k miles there shouldn't be much at all. I think my dad got the car w/ 30k on it and it hasn't seen snow in well over 12 years.<P>Thanks for the help. I think it's going to go to another shop tomorrow- I'm out of town so I have to rely on my dad to get it there.<P>Hopefully, this new shop can get it right.<P>Thanks for the help everyone.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Captain Midnight||08-01-2001||03:37 AM||||Don't grind on the strut tower, grind on the adjustment slots.<P>------------------<BR>Captain Midnight||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||08-01-2001||04:23 AM||||HOLY ****  DO NOT GRIND THE STRUT TOWERS!!!<P>The place to gentley remove a little bit of metal, is on the struts themselves.  Where the two big-arse bolts go.  that's the ajustment area (the hole should already be rather ovloid in shape.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||Fierowrecker||08-01-2001||07:33 AM||||Hey Ferg!<BR>If you have original rubber control arm bushings, they could be the culpret...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wannabe||08-01-2001||03:08 PM||||It's been done many times before with success, I know, but elongating holes in the strut is not the best idea as it can go out of alignment later. A better, although more expensive solution is to use cam bolts. A single cam bolt on each side will provide about 1.5* of camber adjustment. Two bolts on each side gives about 2.5* of adjustment. I'm surprised the shop didn't suggest it. Bolts typically run about $35US per pair.||||reg||1
Z||000011||ferg||08-01-2001||04:07 PM||||I don't really want to grind anything off of the car. If I were to gind anything, it would be the adjustment slots. But I would rather find another alternative.  <P>The car does have the original control arm bushings, to my knowledge. But If that was the cause of the problem, why did the other side align just fine?<P>As for cam bolts- I'm not sure what those are. I'll bring it up at the alignment shop.<P>I'm not worried about the cost, quality is what I'm after.<P>One would think that a Pontiac dealer would have the books to fix these issues. (that's where we took it the first time)<P>It's going into the new alignment shop this evening, will have an update very soon.||||reg||1
